WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:00.900
The Washington Post

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:01.501
now reporting that

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.302
Ginni Thomas,

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.269
the conservative

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.771
activist and firebrand

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.973
and wife of Justice Clarence Thomas,

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.742
received tens of thousands of dollars

00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:11.311
in hidden payments for consulting work.

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.812
The Post reviewed documents

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.180
that showed that the payments to Ginni

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.615
Thomas were set up by conservative

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.317
judicial activist Leonard Leo.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.219
Leonard Leo may not be a household name.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:20.220
Where you are,

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:20.987
but Leonard Leo

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:21.521
helped pick

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.356
every single one of Donald Trump's

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.124
Supreme Court

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:25.258
justice appointments,

00:00:25.258 --> 00:00:27.127
as well as dozens of conservative judges

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:28.094
with lifetime

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:30.063
appointments to the federal bench.

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:30.697
During a Senate

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.766
Judiciary hearing this week on ethics

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:33.600
in the Supreme Court,

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.734
or lack thereof,

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:35.635
Democratic Senator

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:37.704
Sheldon Whitehouse described Leonard Leo

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.639
this way.

00:00:39.639 --> 00:00:40.807
This guy doesn't have business

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:41.841
before the court.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:44.044
His business is the court.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:45.211
According to the Washington Post

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.313
in January 2012.

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:49.082
Leonard Leo told conservative pollster

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:50.817
Kellyanne Conway

00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:51.751
to bill

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:54.487
the Judicial Education Project nonprofit.

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:55.955
Leo then instructed Conway

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:58.758
to give Ginni Thomas another $25,000

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.093
and the documents show, he emphasized,

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:01.995
there should be no mention

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.230
of Ginni Thomas in any paperwork.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.732
Never good

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:06.800
when someone tells you

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.268
not to put something in writing

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.469
and then puts it in writing.

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:10.770
Leo told the Post, quote,

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.472
It is no secret that Ginni Thomas

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:13.773
has a long history of working on issues

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.341
within the conservative movement.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.309
The work she did here

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:17.310
did not involve anything

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.244
connected with

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:19.412
either the court's business

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.147
or with other legal issues, unquote.

00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:21.915
When asked

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.817
why he wanted to keep Ginni

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:26.119
Thomas's name off the paperwork,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.121
Leonard Leo told the Post, quote,

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:28.721
Knowing how

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:31.357
disrespectful, malicious and gossipy

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:32.492
people can be,

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:33.626
I have always tried

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.161
to protect the privacy of Justice

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:36.996
Thomas and Ginni, unquote.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.264
Apparently the belief

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.333
that transparency and accountability

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.768
for the highest court in the land

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.003
are just gauche and rude.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:45.672
Let's bring in CNN's senior

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:46.473
Supreme Court analyst

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:47.173
Joan Biskupic,

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.141
along with the former director

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:49.075
for the Office of Government

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.977
Ethics, Walter Shaub.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:51.811
Joan, let me start with you.

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:52.912
So people out

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.547
there might not realize

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:57.317
how huge a role Leonard Leo has played in

00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:59.452
so much of what the court has done,

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.221
including overturning Roe v Wade

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.255
and turning the.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.623
What we assume is going to be

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:05.058
the dismantling of affirmative action

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:06.426
in higher education.

00:02:06.426 --> 00:02:08.128
Walk us through the kind of influence

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:08.628
he has.

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:11.097
You cannot overstate the influence

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:13.299
of Leonard Leo on the Supreme Court.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:13.566
In fact,

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.002
I've described him as someone who

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:17.370
once you get beyond the White House

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.037
or Congress,

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:20.273
he has had arguably the greatest effect

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:21.274
on our federal bench.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.009
You mentioned the three Trump appointees

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:25.111
that he essentially handpicked

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:26.412
through the years.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.647
He also every single

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.449
Republican appointee on this court

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:30.683
was approved by him

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:31.918
one way or another,

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:32.652
including Chief

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.821
Justice John Roberts, who met with him.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:35.288
John Roberts

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:36.789
probably would have been selected anyway,

00:02:36.789 --> 00:02:38.758
but he did meet with Leonard Leo.

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.994
He is the consummate networker.

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:42.328
The Federalist Society goes

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.464
all the way back to the early 1980s,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.533
but it was in 1991

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:48.968
when Leonard Leo became a director,

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:51.971
that he began raising all sorts of money

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.807
in high school. His nickname was Mr.

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:55.508
Moneybags.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:58.411
He is he is a terrific networker.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:02:59.546
He's very effective.

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:00.947
Anyone on what he wants.

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:01.648
He worked

00:03:01.648 --> 00:03:03.917
very closely with Mitch McConnell

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:07.654
in the Senate leader in selecting people.

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:09.756
And he was very close to Don McGahn

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.391
in the Trump White House.

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:12.592
And most recently,

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:16.396
he secured a $1.6 billion donation

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.231
that will help him be even more powerful

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.399
going forward with money.

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:19.966
So, Walter,

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:20.733
Democratic Congressman

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:21.668
Ted Lieu of California

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:23.002
tweeted, quote,

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.037
Leonard Leo directed

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.438
fake invoices to be made

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:26.606
so that he could secretly line

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:27.574
the pockets of the wife

00:03:27.574 --> 00:03:29.075
of a Supreme Court justice.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.477
This is corrupt, unquote.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.947
How is it corrupt?

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:35.348
Obviously, it doesn't look good,

00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:36.482
but it's not as though Leonard

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.884
Leo is

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.352
secretive about what

00:03:39.352 --> 00:03:40.753
he wants the court to do.

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.322
It's not as though he's

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:43.456
there making decisions

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:44.591
like in the Pelican Brief,

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:45.091
where he stands

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.292
to make millions of dollars

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.461
from from land acquisitions in Florida.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:49.462
I mean, how is it corrupt

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:50.396
if you think it is?

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:50.797
You know,

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.398
I think his own actions

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.234
demonstrate that there was an awareness

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.402
that it at least appears corrupt

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:57.270
because you wouldn't work

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.639
that hard to hide the source of money

00:03:59.639 --> 00:04:00.640
if you didn't worry

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.308
about how it was going to appear.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:03.610
Now he talks about people

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.678
being malicious and gossipy.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:06.946
I think the other way to spin

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:07.313
that is

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:07.914
the public

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.149
is concerned about who's paying members

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.050
of the Supreme Court

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.118
and their spouses

00:04:12.118 --> 00:04:14.020
or rather or their spouses.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:14.320
Yeah.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.121
So we're in an era

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.323
now where there has been more scrutiny

00:04:17.323 --> 00:04:18.725
of the US Supreme Court

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:20.493
probably in the last month or two

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:21.227
because of ProPublica

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:24.664
in the Washington Post and CNN, then,

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:24.964
you know,

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:27.900
it feels then it's history before then.

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.303
We now also have

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.404
there's been some stories

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:33.139
about how two Supreme Court justices,

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:35.041
Sonia Sotomayor and Neil Gorsuch,

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:37.410
did not recuse themselves from cases

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:37.677
that came

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:39.112
before the court over the past decade

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:40.847
involving a specific publisher

00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:42.982
publishing company that has paid them

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.551
lots of money and lucrative book deals.

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:46.886
Former Justice Stephen

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:48.488
Breyer, who's also received royalties

00:04:48.488 --> 00:04:49.455
from the same publisher,

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.958
he did recuse himself.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:52.892
How do you see this?

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:53.159
Okay.

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:53.826
Well, this involves

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:56.496
a case, several cases where Penguin,

00:04:56.663 --> 00:04:58.831
Random House, the conglomerate that both

00:04:58.831 --> 00:04:59.432
all three,

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:01.668
Justice Stephen Breyer, Justice Sonia

00:05:01.668 --> 00:05:03.002
Sotomayor and Neil Gorsuch,

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:03.603
all have

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:04.470
been contracts

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.271
with all contracts

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:07.507
that they have disclosed over time.

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:08.341
And two of them

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.143
did not recuse himself from cases

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:12.345
that were on the list of many,

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:13.713
many petitions coming to the court.

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.548
And this is something that fix the court,

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:16.516
a watchdog group.

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:18.184
And then The Daily Wire had talked

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.253
about, focused

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.055
more on two of the justices.

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:23.289
The reason, Stephen Breyer,

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:24.390
it could have been frankly,

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:26.059
I don't know why they didn't recuse

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:27.226
because they didn't say.

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:28.628
We tried to ask them, you know,

00:05:28.628 --> 00:05:29.162
why didn't

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:30.463
you recuse yourself from cases?

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:30.930
They were cases

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.799
that were not heard by the court.

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:33.700
So it's

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.102
and they did record the report there.

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:37.603
I do want to mention, though,

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:39.105
Jake, that Stephen Breyer,

00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:40.406
who did recuse,

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:41.474
likely didn't recuse

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:43.176
because he had gotten money from this.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.177
You know, a subsidiary

00:05:44.177 --> 00:05:44.977
of the publishing house.

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:46.512
It's because his family hit on

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.481
some stock in the in the publisher

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:52.251
so that this area is not of the magnitude

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.386
of other things. We've talked about.

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:54.854
But what we've tried to do

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.156
is bring all these things to public light

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:57.957
as they've come up,

00:05:57.957 --> 00:05:59.692
because it is important to say

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:01.594
it's not just Clarence Thomas,

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:04.230
even though he's involved so much.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:04.831
Right.

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:05.765
There are other justices

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:06.632
who have had concerns

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.134
because there's also this information

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:08.534
with Mr.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:09.335
Crow,

00:06:09.335 --> 00:06:11.637
who has been funding Thomas,

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:12.438
who's a friend of

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.340
his, finding his vacations,

00:06:15.308 --> 00:06:16.075
buying land

00:06:16.075 --> 00:06:17.610
from his family, and letting Clarence

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.645
Thomas, his mom, live there, helping

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.414
with a kid that a relative

00:06:21.414 --> 00:06:22.648
that that the Thomas family

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.917
has been taking and taking care of.

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.221
Defenders of Thomas and Harlan Grove say

00:06:28.755 --> 00:06:29.722
this is just a friend

00:06:29.722 --> 00:06:31.557
doing a nice thing for another friend.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.292
You know, here's the problem.

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:34.594
We are in a situation

00:06:34.594 --> 00:06:35.495
when the Supreme Court

00:06:35.495 --> 00:06:37.397
has no code of ethics whatsoever.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:38.197
There's no rules on

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:39.832
what kind of gifts you can take.

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:41.367
And there's very little

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:42.835
oversight of their disclosure.

00:06:42.835 --> 00:06:44.070
So they're not even complying

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.205
with the transparency law that there is.

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:49.575
Back in about 2011, just as Chief Justice

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:51.244
Roberts began pushing back

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:52.812
more aggressive of lay

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:55.381
against the idea of a code of ethics.

00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:56.582
I think where we are

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:58.818
now is the inevitable result of that.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.620
It's a proof of the axiom

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.989
that absolute power corrupts absolutely.

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:03.956
These individuals

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:05.691
are not subject to scrutiny.

00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:07.226
They haven't subjected themselves

00:07:07.226 --> 00:07:08.461
to a code of conduct.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:09.929
I think this is the beginning,

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:11.697
not the end of these problems.

00:07:11.697 --> 00:07:13.366
And I saw a tweet

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:15.234
suggesting that, you know,

00:07:15.234 --> 00:07:16.202
one of the issues,

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:16.803
Joan, you know,

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:17.937
I think the best Supreme Court

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:18.704
reporter in the country,

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:19.238
I'm going on the record

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:21.441
for that for the last 22 years.

00:07:21.441 --> 00:07:22.642
But Nina TOTENBERG,

00:07:22.642 --> 00:07:23.876
who is very well respected,

00:07:23.876 --> 00:07:25.645
long time Supreme Court reporter for NPR,

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:26.479
she was criticized

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.548
by NPR's public editor in 2020

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:30.416
for not disclosing

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:31.918
her decades long relationship

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:33.986
with Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg.

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:35.288
And this might suggest

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:36.989
that one of the problems here

00:07:36.989 --> 00:07:38.958
is a lot of the journalists

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:40.059
who have been covering the Supreme

00:07:40.059 --> 00:07:41.594
Court, again, not you and

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:43.029
and not a bunch of other

00:07:43.029 --> 00:07:44.430
really excellent Supreme Court reporters

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.232
like Jan Crawford and others.

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:48.234
But but some on the right

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:50.036
and the left have formed such close,

00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:51.471
close relationships

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:52.572
that you really have to wonder

00:07:52.572 --> 00:07:54.674
about their journalism sometimes.

00:07:54.841 --> 00:07:55.141
Yeah,

00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:57.643
it is a pretty intimate group of people.

00:07:57.643 --> 00:07:59.479
I always kid that justices are appointed

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:00.079
for life.

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.848
Journalists are appointed for life, too.

00:08:01.848 --> 00:08:02.782
We come to this beat

00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:03.783
and we don't leave it.

00:08:03.783 --> 00:08:05.251
But I mean, like anything else,

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:06.219
you want to be friendly

00:08:06.219 --> 00:08:07.353
with the people you cover,

00:08:07.353 --> 00:08:08.754
but you don't want to be deep friends.

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:10.923
And if you have deep friendships, then

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.291
then you try to,

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:13.626
you know, be careful with

00:08:13.626 --> 00:08:15.161
with your coverage on that.

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:16.195
And I do think that

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:17.630
there has been an insularity

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.232
that we've we fight against.

00:08:19.232 --> 00:08:21.334
And it's been important to always be able

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.135
to scrutinize these justices

00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:24.837
in various ways, which, frankly,

00:08:24.837 --> 00:08:26.239
I think many of us have.

00:08:26.239 --> 00:08:27.940
But these recent stories,

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.009
they're coming from investigative teams

00:08:30.009 --> 00:08:30.877
really strong.

00:08:30.877 --> 00:08:31.911
And I want to give credit

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:33.012
where credit is due.

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:35.248
Really strong investigative teams,

00:08:35.248 --> 00:08:36.549
especially at ProPublica.

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:36.782
Yeah.

00:08:36.782 --> 00:08:38.284
You know, and we're those people

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:39.719
they may never have even set foot

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:40.419
in the Supreme Court

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:41.454
to cover a case

00:08:41.454 --> 00:08:42.221
where someone like me

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:42.855
is going to be in there

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:44.323
reading the briefs all the time.

00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:45.091
And again,

00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:46.592
I think the more the more coverage

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:47.260
we have, the better.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:48.094
Yeah, more coverage

00:08:48.094 --> 00:08:49.795
and better and more transparent.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:50.229
See, I don't know

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:51.797
if they're listening here at the US

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:52.331
Supreme Court.

00:08:52.331 --> 00:08:54.500
More transparency.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:55.034
Walter Sharp

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:56.302
and Joan Biskupic, thanks to both of you.

